  "answer": "I'll start by exploring the workspace.\nThe bug is clear: `add` subtracts instead of adding. Let me confirm the failure first, then fix.\nConfirmed failing (`add(2,3)` → `-1`). Now the minimal fix:\nFixed and verified.\n\n## Summary\n\n**Bug:** In `src/add.ts`, the `add` function used subtraction (`a - b`) instead of addition, so every result was off by `2 * b`.\n\n**Reproduced:** `bun test test/oracle.test.ts` failed on the first case — `add(2, 3)` returned `-1` where `5` was expected.\n\n**Fix:** A one-token change in the operator, `a - b` → `a + b`:\n\n```ts\nexport function add(a: number, b: number) { return a + b }\n```\n\n**Preserved as required:**\n- Public signature `(a: number, b: number) => number` is unchanged — no call-site or type impact.\n- `test/oracle.test.ts`, `package.json`, and `AGENTS.md` untouched; no config added and the runner (Bun's built-in `bun:test`) is still the one in use.\n\n**Verification:** The existing suite now passes — 1 test, 5 expect calls covering positive, mixed-sign, zero, negative-negative, and carry cases (e.g. `add(99, 1) === 100`).\n\nWhy the original failure only showed up on some inputs is worth noting: subtraction happens to agree with addition when `b === 0` (the `[0, 0, 0]` case would have passed), so a weaker test would have missed this. The signed-input loop in the existing test is what caught it."
